<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Huron Confederacy</h1><p class="st-meta">1440 CE – 1650 CE (approx.) · capital Ossossane</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An alliance of Wendat nations between Lake Simcoe and Georgian Bay, the hub of the early French fur trade until Iroquois attacks dispersed it in 1649.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Wendat tradition holds that the Bear and Cord nations formed a league around 1440, joined by the Rock nation around 1590 and the Deer around 1610. Their country, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huronia">Wendake</a> or Huronia, lay in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simcoe-county">Simcoe County</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, where 20,000 to 30,000 people lived in palisaded villages of longhouses, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossossane">Ossossane</a>, the main town of the Bear.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A confederacy council of village and clan chiefs met to settle disputes and decide on war and trade, and the Feast of the Dead united communities through shared reburial of their dead. Women controlled fields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and squash, whose surplus the Wendat traded north to Algonquian hunters for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>. Allied with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-france">New France</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Champlain</a>'s visit in 1615, they hosted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sainte-marie-among-the-hurons">Sainte-Marie among the Hurons</a>, founded in 1639.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Epidemics in the 1630s halved the population, and in 1648 to 1649 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy</a> destroyed the main towns and killed missionaries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-de-brebeuf">Jean de Brébeuf</a>. Survivors fled; some settled near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-quebec">Quebec</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huronia">Wendake</a> survives, and others joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petun">Petun</a> to become the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wyandot">Wyandot</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/huron-confederacy.jpg" alt="Huron Confederacy" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pair_of_Moccasins_MET_DT258622.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1440 CE – 1650 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ossossane</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Wendat traditional religion, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Wyandot</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Canada</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wendat Confederacy, Wendake, Huron</dd></dl></aside></div>

